NOTE ON CIVIL PROCEDURE AND THE 1L CURRICULUM
We’ve considered the importance of procedure to legal practice. We’ve started reading cases of procedure. But we have yet to define “procedure.” Further, civil procedure is but one course in your 1L year. Perhaps you are also taking torts, contracts, property, constitutional law, or criminal law. If those courses are not about procedure, what are they about?

Consider the following characterization of procedural law and its counterpart, substantive law: Substantive law determines which acts are permitted and which are forbidden, defining what relief is available to an aggrieved party and what that party must prove to obtain relief. Procedural law defines the steps by which a party goes about bringing a claim to court and ultimately to judgment on that claim. Tort law tells us who would be liable in the event of a chemical spill at a factory. But when a chemical spill happens, legal relief does not shower down from heaven. The victims must seek out redress for the wrongs they have suffered. Procedure tells us when, where, and how they can sue the the factory owner for their injuries. Only through procedure do the legal rights and obligations created by substantive law become the reality of monetary compensation, cleanup of the spill, medical care, or something else.

In this way, procedure and substantive law may be distinct concepts, but they are bound together in the real-world practice of law. Procedure is how the substantive law is given real world meaning and effect. Karl Llewellyn, in his canonical introduction to the law, offers you invaluable advice:

You must read each substantive course, so to speak, through the spectacles of … procedure. For what substantive law says should be means nothing except in terms of what procedure says that you can make real.

KARL LLEWELLYN, THE BRAMBLE BUSH 9 (3d ed. 1960).

