Civil Procedure in Brief
Learning civil procedure can be difficult because it involves many interrelated and connected subtopics—what lawyers sometimes called the “seamless web” of civil procedure. Often, your study of one area of civil procedure will involve rules and doctrines from other areas of civil procedure. To address this challenge, the remainder of this chapter introduces key concepts and context for your study of civil procedure. It will provide brief sketches of the topics in each of the following chapters in this book. This introduction to civil procedure may seem like a whirlwind, but studying these introductory notes and the overview of civil procedure will make the rest of your studies easier.

As you read the materials in this section, you will see in bold face key legal terms of art—in other words, words with specialized meanings when used in legal contexts. Take note of them! These terms are part of every litigation attorney’s vocabulary, and we will encounter them throughout this book. Do not worry if you cannot remember all of them or if you do not understand the meaning of a term. We will study them in detail in the chapters that follow. These introductory notes are merely an opportunity to see the rough outline of civil procedure and begin to familiarize yourself with its jargon.
SOURCES OF AMERICAN LAW AND THE STRUCTURE OF AMERICAN COURTS
There are many court systems in the United States: the courts of 50 states and the federal courts, for starters. (There are also tribal courts and, in U.S. territories, territorial courts. The distinct procedural and jurisdictional rules of these courts are beyond the scope of this book. For an excellent introduction to tribal courts and tribal law, see Elizabeth A. Reese, The Other American Law, 73 STAN. L. REV. 555 (2021).) Each of these court systems has its own court rules and its own judges. Each state has its own laws. The federal government has its own laws, many of which overlap with state laws, but all of which are distinct precisely because they are the authoritative commands of different sovereigns. State courts primarily, but not exclusively, adjudicate claims brought under state law. Federal courts primarily, but not exclusively, adjudicate claims brought under federal law.

47
Given all this, it is important to be precise when referring to laws and courts in the United States. This book will use the terms United States courts and United States law to refer solely to the courts and laws of the United States government—in other words, the federal government. Thus, you should treat the terms federal courts and United States courts as interchangeable, and federal law and United States law as interchangeable. (And note that when this book says “the Constitution,” it means the United States Constitution.) These terms will be used in contrast to state courts and state law, which refer solely to the institutions of the each of the 50 states that comprise the United States. When this book refers to the federal and state courts together, it will use the term American courts. Likewise, American law refers to federal and state law together. To be clear, the terms “American courts” and “American law” do not generally have this meaning in contexts other than this book. This book uses these terms to avoid the need to repeatedly clarify whether it means to treat state and federal law together or separately. As we shall discover, it is very important to keep clear the distinction between state courts and federal courts, and state law and federal law, when studying civil procedure!

Now let’s take a moment to review the basics of the sources of American law and the structure of the American courts. Note that this book’s primary focus will be federal civil procedure and federal court practice. This is because federal courts and federal procedure have nationwide scope and are relevant to legal practice in every state, and because federal procedure has been influential on state court procedure. For example, the main procedural rules promulgated by the federal courts, the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, apply only in federal court, but many states have adopted rules of civil procedure for their state courts that closely mirror the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure in some or most respects.

Overview of the Federal Courts

The federal court system has three tiers. The district courts comprise the first tier. They are courts of first impression—in other words, these are the courts where cases begin. The federal district courts are organized into geographical districts, each of which corresponds to all or part of a single state (or Puerto Rico or the District of Columbia). A civil lawsuit in federal court is called a civil action, and district court is where a plaintiff commences a civil action and where the plaintiff and defendant litigate their dispute, possibly all the way to trial. For this reason, district courts (and their equivalents in state
48
court) are sometimes called trial courts, even though most cases do not end in trial. (Most cases end in settlement.) Each civil action is heard by a single district judge, although each district court usually has more than one district judge.

Most of what happens in federal litigation happens in the district courts. As noted above, litigation begins with a plaintiff filing suit in district court. Lawyers for the parties then file appearances (formal notices informing the court that they are representing a party), and parties without lawyers appear pro se (i.e., they represent themselves). The defendant might ask the district court to dismiss the case. The parties might attempt to negotiate a settlement. And it is in district court that court procedures allow parties to collect evidence from each other and from third parties (i.e., people who are not parties to the action). The parties then will present this evidence, together with arguments about the law, to the court and ask the court to enter a judgment in their favor. A district court’s decision in a case will depend on findings of fact (the judge’s or jury’s resolution of disputes over facts—for example, when the defendant drove into the intersection, was the traffic light red, or was it green?—based on the evidence presented) and conclusions of law (the judge’s statement of the legal principles governing how the case should be decided).

The courts of appeals are the second tier. They are organized into circuits. Twelve circuits are geographic in scope, and each district falls within one of the twelve geographic circuits. (The thirteenth is the Federal Circuit, which hears certain specialized appeals, including patent law cases from all district courts.) The geographic arrangement of the circuit courts and the districts within them is illustrated in the figure below.

A party who loses in the district court has the right to an appeal, in which that party, the appellant, asks the court of appeals to change the district court’s decision. The party who prevailed in the district court (the appellee) will defend the district court’s decision on appeal. The United States courts of appeals sit in panels of three judges who decide the appeal by majority vote after considering the arguments of the parties. A party disappointed by the decision of an appellate panel may ask the court of appeals for rehearing and may even request that all of the judges of the court appeals sit together to rehear the appeal; this is called rehearing en banc. Depending the circuit, this could involve as few as six judges or as many as 30. Rehearing is rare in the United States circuit courts.
The third tier is the United States Supreme Court, which this book will sometimes refer to as simply the “Supreme Court” or the “Court” (with a capital “C”). In most civil actions, parties have no right to appeal to the Supreme Court. Instead, they must ask the Court to exercise its discretion to hear the case. This request is made through a court filing called a petition for certiorari. The party seeking review in the Supreme Court is called the petitioner, and the party who prevailed in the court of appeals is called the respondent. The Supreme Court is the court of last resort in the federal system, meaning that there is no appeal or petition beyond the Supreme Court.

All of the federal courts described above (plus some others, omitted for simplicity) are known as Article III courts, meaning that their creation is authorized by Article III of the United States Constitution, which creates the “judicial Power of the United States.” Judges in Article III courts are appointed by the President, confirmed by the Senate, and serve for life. There are other courts created by the federal government, however, that adjudicate specialized matters for administrative agencies (immigration courts are an example) or specialized areas of federal law (bankruptcy courts are an example). These courts are known as Article I courts, as they are created under Congress’s legislative powers in Article I of the Constitution. Judges in these courts are not subject to the process of presidential appointment and senate confirmation, and they
50
do not serve for life. These courts have their own procedures and are outside the scope of this book, but it is worth noting that they handle an enormous volume of litigation, some of which eventually finds its way into the Article III courts when parties seek review of the decisions of Article I courts. Finally, some cases in Article III courts are heard by Article I judges who are specially appointed to participate in the work of the district courts. These judges are called magistrate judges. Although their powers are limited—they cannot oversee a trial without the consent of the parties, for example—magistrate judges often take primary responsibility for the pre-trial stages of the civil litigation process.

Most state court systems mirror the three-tiered federal system, though some lack an intermediate appellate court. Be careful, though, when reading state-court opinions, because the tiers of the state court system may have different names than their federal counterparts. For example, Illinois’ trial court is called the Circuit Court, and its intermediate appellate court is called the District Court. New York’s general trial court is called the Supreme Court, its intermediate appellate court is called the Supreme Court, Appellate Division (often simply referred to as the Appellate Division), and its highest court—what in most states would be called the Supreme Court—is called the Court of Appeals. Finally, many states also have a variety of specialized lower courts that handle specific types of cases. Traffic and small claims court are prominent examples.
