Overview of Sources of Law

We now turn from a review of the American court systems to a review of sources of American law. The supreme law of the United States is of course the Constitution. The Constitution, in turn, authorizes the United States government to enact statutes and enter into treaties. Federal statutes are codified in the United States Code (“U.S.C.” for short) and we shall read many sections of the United States Code in this book. The federal government also has many administrative agencies, and Congress has authorized many of these agencies to create regulations which have the force of law.

52
Congress has also authorized the Supreme Court to enact rules governing court procedure and the presentation of evidence at trial. Most relevant to this book are the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure and the Federal Rules of Evidence. The Federal Rules of Civil Procedure provide the ground rules for civil actions in the federal district courts, and this book will devote much attention to them. For brevity, it will often refer to the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure as the “Federal Rules” or the “Rules.” As we study the Federal Rules, always keep in mind that they govern only civil actions in federal court. There are separate rules governing criminal prosecutions in federal court, and state courts have their own rules of procedure, which govern actions filed in state court.

All of this is relatively abstract. We will be spending a lot of time with the Rules, so it may help to be more concrete by reading one of them. Rule 7 is an example of a typical Rule.
