So far, every source of law we have listed has been a form of codified law—laws created through a formal process, authorized by the Constitution or by statute, for the drafting and enactment of legal rules. The archetypical example of codified law is legislation. Other forms of codified law, such as administrative regulations and court rules, likewise take form through a deliberative process of drafting, debate, revision, and enactment by a lawmaking body. Such rules are called “codified law” because they are published in legal codes (such as the United States Code for statutes and the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure for procedural rules).

But there is another source of law, common law, which comes from a different source: court judgments. There are some legal rules that appear nowhere in the text of a constitution, statute, regulation, or court rule. Rather, they are rules that emerged out of the process of English judges—long before the United States or the Constitution existed—deciding disputes that came to them and gradually settling upon a set of rules for deciding those disputes. For example, there is no federal or state statute specifying what the elements of a claim for breach of contract are. The rules for when a contract is formed and what counts as a breach of contract were developed in judicial opinions, the (usually written) statements of judges explaining their decisions in cases. American judges inherited the tradition of common law judging from England, and even today many legal claims are based on common law rules, not statutes or other codified law. The distinction between codified law and common law will become salient at various points in this book.

Although the focus in this discussion has been federal law, every source of federal law has its analogue in state law. This is true even for the Federal Rules. Although the Rules bind only federal courts, they have heavily influenced the states, and many, though not all, states have adopted versions of many of the Rules for their own courts. Further, the role of common law in the creation of legal rules is even larger in the state courts than in the federal courts. As we
54
shall see, the federal court’s ability to create and develop common law rules is far more constrained than the state courts’ ability to do so.

LAW AND EQUITY
As noted above, common law traces its roots back to England—medieval England, in fact. At that time, the “common law” had a more precise definition: it was the law developed by a specific set of courts in England, called the courts of law. One might ask, “Isn’t every court a court of law?,” but in medieval England, the answer was no. Most importantly for this book, there was another set of courts, the courts of equity, and the distinction between actions “at law” (which were heard by the law courts) and actions “in equity” (which were heard by the equity courts) has had a profound influence on the development of American procedure (and is even enshrined in the United States Constitution). For this reason, a brief history of law and equity is in order.

In medieval England, before the equity courts were created, a person with a grievance would petition the king for relief, and the king or his clerks would issue writs customized to the dispute directing the law courts to resolve it in a certain way. The law courts awarded only damages (in other words, money) as a remedy for the plaintiff’s injury. Over time, Parliament restricted the authority of the king to issue new writs. As a result, the courts at law became inflexible and highly formalistic. For example, there could be only one plaintiff and one defendant. The plaintiff was required to select from the set of writs that had been issued in the past the specific type of writ that best matched the facts and type of his case. If he chose the wrong writ, he lost, even if a different writ would have resulted in victory.

Equity developed as an alternative to this rigid system. It arose out of the power of the Chancellor (a powerful official who answered to the king) to grant relief to aggrieved parties in the event that their case did not fit into one of the rigid categories of the writs in the law courts, or if damages was not a suitable remedy for the plaintiff’s injury. Eventually, England established a system of equity courts, also called courts of chancery, with judges called chancellors. The equity courts were more flexible than the law courts, and they existed precisely to give relief when there was no adequate remedy at law. In contrast to the law courts, which could only award damages, the equity courts could award

